  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Let $p$ and $q$ be the roots of the quadratic $x^2+3x+k$ and suppose $p^3q+pq^3=4$. Find all possible values of $k$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=\\frac{1}{2}$ or $4$.\n\nUse $pq(p^2+q^2)=k(9-2k)=4$.",
      "worked": "By Vieta’s formulas, $p+q=-3$ and $pq=k$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}p^2+q^2&=(p+q)^2-2pq\\\\p^2+q^2&=9-2k.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}p^3q+pq^3&=pq(p^2+q^2)\\\\p^3q+pq^3&=k(9-2k).\\end{align*}$$\nUsing the given value,\n$$\\begin{align*}k(9-2k)&=4,\\\\2k^2-9k+4&=0,\\\\(2k-1)(k-4)&=0,\\\\k&=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ or }4.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 92
  },
